# Tech Specs Agent — Architecture & Implementation Plan

## 1. Context & Problem Statement

The SDLC project automates the software development lifecycle. Today, when a user uploads a requirements document (PDF / DOCX / TXT):

1. **Text Extraction Agent** — parses the raw document into clean text.
2. **Requirements Agent** — converts that text into structured **User Stories + Acceptance Criteria**.

These two agents already exist and are fully wired into the pipeline.

**What's missing:** Once user stories are generated, the next natural SDLC step is to produce a **Technical Specification** — a detailed engineering blueprint that maps each user story into concrete technical decisions: architecture patterns, data models, API contracts, technology choices, component breakdowns, and security/performance considerations.

### Goal

Build a new **`tech-specs-agent`** that:

- Receives the **parsed requirement text** and the **generated user stories** (output of the `requirements-agent`).
- Produces a structured **Technical Specification Document** (Markdown) covering architecture, data model, APIs, components, non-functional requirements, and risks.
- Follows the same agent pattern already established in the codebase (LangGraph + HuggingFace LLM + Pydantic validation).
- Plugs into the existing MCP Host ([host.py](file:///c:/Users/Vishal.bhardwaj/SDLC/Agentic_Sys/Agentic_Sys/mcp-host/host.py)) and Java backend ([McpAgentService.java](file:///c:/Users/Vishal.bhardwaj/SDLC/backend-java/src/main/java/com/sdlc/service/McpAgentService.java)) with zero changes to the host infrastructure.

